Reject the empty-bucket sentinel key in the inspector agents' id maps.

The inspector debugger and runtime agents parse a scriptId (or injected
script id) from a protocol string and look it up in an UncheckedKeyHashMap.
parseIntegerAllowingTrailingJunk(...).value_or(0) turns a string that does
not parse (for example "", "abc", "-1", a lone surrogate) into 0. A real
SourceID is never 0 (noSourceID == 0) and injected script ids start at 1,
so 0 is the map's empty-bucket value. The maps are UncheckedKeyHashMap, so
key validation is compiled out, and find(0) on a populated map matches an
empty bucket instead of returning end(). The zero-initialised value is then
used: Debugger.setBreakpoint and Debugger.getBreakpointLocations dereference
a null sourceProvider and crash the debuggee, and Debugger.getScriptSource
and Debugger.searchInContent return an empty source instead of an error.

The fix rejects the sentinel right after parsing, so these commands return
the normal "Missing script" error. This can be dropped once the change
lands in oven-sh/WebKit and WEBKIT_VERSION is bumped past it.

--- a/Source/JavaScriptCore/inspector/InjectedScriptManager.cpp
+++ b/Source/JavaScriptCore/inspector/InjectedScriptManager.cpp
@@ -115,6 +115,12 @@ InjectedScript InjectedScriptManager::injectedScriptForObjectId(const String& ob
     if (!injectedScriptId)
         return InjectedScript();
 
+    // Injected script ids are handed out from m_nextInjectedScriptId starting
+    // at 1, so 0 is never a real id. It is also the empty-bucket value of the
+    // id map, so a lookup of 0 matches an empty bucket in a populated map.
+    if (*injectedScriptId < 1)
+        return InjectedScript();
+
     return m_idToInjectedScript.get(*injectedScriptId);
 }
 
